;; PCI configuration space, read half.
;;
;; This is the first MECHANISM to leave C rather than another decision. Every
;; driver in this kernel starts here: `aiueos_pci_enumerate` walks the bus by
;; calling this, and a BAR, a capability list and an MSI-X table are all just
;; further reads of the same window. So it is the right root to move.
;;
;; It is expressible only because `kernel-in-u32` now exists. Configuration
;; space is not memory-mapped on this path: it is reached by writing a 32-bit
;; address word to port 0xCF8 (3320) and then READING the data word back from
;; port 0xCFC (3324). With write-only port I/O the second step had no spelling
;; in Kotoba at all, which is why the port arithmetic stayed in C until now.
;;
;; The address word (PCI 2.3 sec. 3.2.2.3.2):
;;
;;   bit 31     enable            0x80000000 = 2147483648
;;   bits 30:24 reserved, zero
;;   bits 23:16 bus              (* bus 65536)
;;   bits 15:11 device           (* dev 2048)
;;   bits 10:8  function         (* fn-index 256)
;;   bits 7:2   register number  (bit-and offset 252)
;;   bits 1:0   must be zero -- config space is addressed in dwords, so the
;;              low two bits of the caller's byte offset are masked off here.
;;              A caller wanting one byte reads the dword and shifts; that is
;;              what `config8` in kernel/pci.c already does.
;;
;; The fields are disjoint by construction, so `bit-or` and `+` would agree.
;; `bit-or` is written because the word is a bit layout, not a sum.

;; DOMAIN AND FAILURE (the decision this object now owns)
;;
;; The four inputs are checked against the widths of their fields -- bus <= 255,
;; dev <= 31, fn <= 7, offset <= 255 -- and an out-of-domain call performs NO
;; port I/O and returns 0xFFFFFFFF (4294967295).
;;
;; Doing no I/O is the point, not the return value. The fields are adjacent in
;; a single word and only the offset is masked, so an over-wide field does not
;; fail -- it CARRIES into its neighbour and the access silently lands on a
;; real, different device:
;;
;;   dev 32 -> bit 16, the low bit of the BUS field: reads bus 1 device 0.
;;   fn 8   -> bit 11, the low bit of the DEVICE field: reads device 1 fn 0.
;;   bus 256-> bit 24, one of the reserved bits 30:24, which the host bridge
;;             is under no obligation to ignore.
;;
;; The offset is the one field that cannot carry, because `bit-and offset 252`
;; already truncates it -- but it fails in its own way, silently wrapping: 256
;; masks to 0 and reads register 0, and a NEGATIVE offset masks to 252 and
;; reads register 63. A wrong register in the right function is a milder
;; failure than a wrong device, which is why it is checked with the others but
;; is not the reason the check exists.
;;
;; A trap would be safer still, but there is no trap in this subset, and a
;; probe that halts the boot is a worse failure than one that reports nothing
;; there.
;;
;; 0xFFFFFFFF is chosen because it is what the host bridge itself returns for
;; an absent function, so every existing caller already handles it: the
;; enumeration loop tests `id0 == 0xffffffffU` and moves on. The object fails
;; closed into a path the callers already have, rather than inventing a code
;; they would ignore.
;;
;; The C wrapper's `uint8_t` parameters already bound bus and offset at 255 --
;; and the check is still made here, because the wrapper is mechanism with no
;; authority over the decision, and because dev <= 31 and fn <= 7 are not
;; expressible as a C type at all. The negative comparisons are not reachable
;; through today's `uint8_t` wrapper either; they are what makes this object
;; total over its own i64 signature rather than over its current caller.

(defn aiueos-pci-config-read [bus dev fn-index offset]
  (if (or (< bus 0) (> bus 255)
          (< dev 0) (> dev 31)
          (< fn-index 0) (> fn-index 7)
          (< offset 0) (> offset 255))
    4294967295
    ;; `let` here is sequencing, not naming: the binding is evaluated exactly
    ;; once, in order, before the body -- so the address word reaches 0xCF8
    ;; before 0xCFC is read, which is the entire protocol. The written value is
    ;; deliberately unused.
    (let [addressed (kernel-out-u32 3320
                                    (bit-or 2147483648
                                            (bit-or (* bus 65536)
                                                    (bit-or (* dev 2048)
                                                            (bit-or (* fn-index 256)
                                                                    (bit-and offset 252))))))]
      (kernel-in-u32 3324))))

(defn main [] 0)
